WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
Cloud PII protection has to follow the data lifecycle, not just the primary database.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:19.400
A practical lifecycle view includes collection, transfer, ingestion, storage, access, use, transformation, replication, sharing, archival, return, and deletion.

3
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:33.260
At each stage, the organization should know what PII exists, why it is present, who can access it, where it is stored, which copies exist, how long it remains, and what evidence proves that the intended control operated.

4
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:36.420
Inventory and data-flow mapping are the starting points.

5
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:51.800
The provider and customer need enough understanding to classify PII, identify sources and destinations, map service regions, describe tenant separation, and distinguish production data from test, diagnostic, and support artifacts.

6
00:00:51.900 --> 00:01:02.380
Metadata and logs deserve attention because they can contain IP addresses, identifiers, message content, search strings, object names, or support notes that relate to a person.

7
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.760
Treating logs as non-PII by default is a common implementation mistake.

8
00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.440
Access and use decisions should be linked to lifecycle context.

9
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:23.420
A support engineer viewing customer PII, an administrator restoring a backup, an automated process generating analytics, and a customer exporting data are different activities.

10
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:30.480
Each needs an owner, authorization path, access control, logging, retention rule, and evidence.

11
00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:34.740
Temporary processing files and derived data should not be invisible.

12
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:47.000
If a transformation produces indexes, aggregates, embeddings, previews, or reports that can still relate to individuals, those artifacts should be included in the privacy design.

13
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.280
Deletion is one of the hardest lifecycle topics in cloud services.

14
00:01:51.380 --> 00:02:03.220
Deleting a primary database record may not immediately remove backups, replicas, search indexes, caches, logs, analytics stores, support attachments, temporary exports, or derived datasets.

15
00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:19.560
A defensible deletion design describes retention triggers, retention periods, legal holds, deletion methods, propagation rules, backup-expiration behavior, verification methods, exceptions, customer-facing commitments, and evidence.

16
00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:29.980
ISO/IEC 27555 can be useful supplementary guidance for organizations developing PII deletion policies and procedures.

17
00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:34.660
Return and portability of customer data also need operational design.

18
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:48.440
At termination or migration, a customer may need to export data in a usable format, confirm what remains during a transition period, understand when residual copies expire, and know how deletion is verified.

19
00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:55.180
A provider should avoid promising instant elimination if the architecture includes backups or delayed propagation.

20
00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:01.280
A better commitment explains the process, timing, limitations, exceptions, and evidence.

21
00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:11.960
PII-principal requests, such as access, correction, deletion, export, objection, or restriction, depend on applicable requirements and roles.

22
00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:24.060
In many cloud processor relationships, the customer is the party that responds directly to the individual, while the provider supports the customer through tools, procedures, or cooperation commitments.

23
00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:33.440
The provider should not assume it always responds directly to the person, and the customer should not assume the provider can satisfy every request without customer action.

24
00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:38.180
Lifecycle management is where privacy promises become traceable operations.

25
00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.900
Lifecycle review should also account for service integration.

26
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:52.560
Many cloud services connect to identity providers, customer-managed storage, third-party analytics, ticketing systems, email gateways, and data warehouses.

27
00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:57.780
PII can move across those connections even when the main product screen hides the complexity.

28
00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:06.120
Integration inventories, data-transfer controls, and customer configuration guidance help prevent accidental expansion of the processing environment.

29
00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:15.900
When the customer controls an integration, the provider can still support safer use by documenting data fields, default settings, retention implications, and logging behavior.

30
00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:20.820
Lifecycle evidence should be generated by the process, not invented afterward.

31
00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:29.640
Retention jobs, export workflows, deletion queues, backup-expiration logs, and support-case closures can all produce records.

32
00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:35.020
Those records help customers and auditors distinguish designed behavior from undocumented best effort.

33
00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:46.960
They also help engineering teams improve the service, because failed jobs, late queues, unusual exports, and exception patterns become visible operational signals rather than hidden privacy debt.
